proposal.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [
    {
      "name": "lifted_count",
      "description": "When several highlighted cubes remained after the button press, the stated red-before-green order conflicted with a recorded green-first pickup. After a red pickup, the stated increasing-y order also conflicted with picking the highlighted blue cube nearer y=0 before the farther robot-right blue cube. These are SUFFICIENCY failures in interpreting the count and PREDICTABILITY failures when a completed lift is excluded because it is not next in the stated order. Replace the fixed ordering everywhere in this field with green, red, blue, omitting unhighlighted colors; within each color use decreasing original world y, then increasing original world x for ties. Derive identities and original positions from highlight_frame, never from positions changed by handling. The field remains the length of the completed prefix of this order, initially 0. Increment only at the first cycle observing the next target held off the table after a completed lift to approximately TCP z=0.15. Do not increment at grasp or when commanding the lift, and do not decrement on return or release. For example, after the highlighted green cube has been lifted and before the highlighted red cube has been lifted, write 1.",
      "value_type": "count"
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [
    {
      "name": "lifted_count",
      "description": "The previous ordering caused the count to exclude a visibly completed green-first lift and selected the wrong remaining blue cube after a red lift. This breaks SUFFICIENCY and makes the prescribed memory update inconsistent with observed successful pickups, breaking PREDICTABILITY. Recover a fixed target order from highlight_frame using green, red, blue, omitting unhighlighted colors; within each color use decreasing original world y, then increasing original world x for ties. Keep identities anchored to that frame rather than reordering handled cubes. Initialize lifted_count to 0. Increment by one only when this cycle's motion frames and current held-cube evidence show the next identity after the carried completed prefix has completed a lift to approximately TCP z=0.15. A low grasp, merely commanding a lift, an empty grasp, an action executing no steps, or a previously counted cube still held does not increment it. Preserve the value through release and retreat. For example, write 1 when the first highlighted green cube's completed lift is observed, even though a highlighted red cube remains."
    }
  ],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": "When choosing among multiple highlighted cubes after pressing the button, the color and within-color ordering rules sent the hand to a different cube from the recorded target. Memory correctly retained the highlight frame and a completed-lift count, but its stated ordering did not support the required waypoint, a SUFFICIENCY failure. Replace every red-green-blue ordering clause with green-red-blue, omitting unhighlighted colors, and replace increasing world y within a color with decreasing original world y, using increasing original world x for ties. Use the same order as the memory instructions, anchored to highlight_frame. Thus a highlighted green cube precedes a highlighted red cube, and among highlighted blue cubes the more positive-y cube precedes the more negative-y cube. Select the next uncounted identity before testing alignment. If the open hand is already within the existing hover tolerance above that identity, descend to its center at z=0.02 and close rather than moving to another cube. Preserve the existing completed-lift, return, release, and retreat rules. Separately, initial button approach and post-button cube approach sometimes selected the correct operation and identity but placed the waypoint more than 1 cm away because raised visible surfaces were treated as table-plane points. This is a geometric SUFFICIENCY failure, not missing historical memory. Add an explicit calibrated localization rule: back-project a visible surface center onto that surface's horizontal height plane, not automatically onto z=0. Estimate the height and center from object geometry and both calibrated current views where available. For the button use the raised circular pressing surface, not the white base or an assumed table-plane center. For a resting cube use its top-face center at the cube's top height, then use the resulting world x,y for the hover and grasp waypoint. Infer cube yaw by reconstructing top-face edge directions in the horizontal world plane, not by copying their image angles. Replace the mandatory yaw restriction to approximately [-0.7854,0.7854] with selection of an edge-aligned square-equivalent yaw requiring the least rotation from the current hand yaw; preserve that alignment during descent and lift. These localization and orientation rules add no memory fields because the relevant geometry is visible in the current calibrated views.",
  "refused": "Do not adopt the report that the highlighted cube beneath the already-aligned hand was red rather than green. The highlight image, current wrist image, low grasp, and subsequent lift consistently show a green cube being picked first, followed later by the highlighted red cube at a different position. Relabeling the green cube as red would conceal the actual ordering error and contradict the observed episode. Retain the report's valid operational conclusion—an aligned open hand should descend and close—but resolve target identity through the corrected shared order, not a color reassignment. No new stored target-position or orientation field is needed for the localization reports: those quantities are recoverable from current calibrated geometry."
}

optimizer.text:
{
  "annotator_prompt": [],
  "policy_prompt_for_memory": [],
  "policy_prompt_for_action": [
    {
      "modify_from": "Interpret all positions in world coordinates using the supplied camera calibration; estimate button and cube locations from their geometry, not raw image left/right. Use the wrist view to refine alignment.",
      "modify_to": "Interpret all positions in world coordinates using the supplied camera calibration; estimate button and cube locations from their geometry, not raw image left/right. Back-project a visible surface center onto that surface's horizontal height plane, not automatically onto z=0. Estimate surface height and center from object geometry and both calibrated current views where available. For the button, localize the raised circular pressing surface rather than its white base. For a resting cube, localize the top-face center at the cube's top height. Use the resulting world x,y for object-centered approach and contact waypoints, retaining the separately specified TCP waypoint heights. Use the wrist view to refine alignment. Approximate hover alignment permits the next operation but does not make the achieved TCP x,y the object center."
    },
    {
      "modify_from": "Use roll=3.1416, pitch=0 and a yaw aligned to its horizontal square edges, choosing the equivalent square orientation in approximately [-0.7854,0.7854].",
      "modify_to": "Use roll=3.1416 and pitch=0. Reconstruct the cube's top-face edge directions in the horizontal world plane using camera calibration and the top-face height; do not copy image edge angles as world yaw. Among the square-equivalent edge-aligned yaws, choose the one requiring the least rotation from the current hand yaw. Preserve that alignment during descent and lift."
    }
  ],
  "refused": "Do not replace the shared red-green-blue, increasing-original-world-y ordering with green-red-blue, decreasing-original-world-y ordering. Round 1 explicitly established robot-right-to-left same-color pickups, and its committed correction reduced waypoint failures from 16 to 6 without memory errors. This round supplies evidence of conflicting pickups but no observable condition distinguishing the two orders. A global reversal would settle this round by reinstating the earlier failure. Retain the existing shared ordering pending evidence of a cycle-observable selection rule that explains both sets of pickups; neither a new fixed order nor an additional memory field can supply that missing rule. Consequently the reported ordering failures remain unresolved rather than being concealed by a contradictory edit. Do not relabel the observed green cube as red. Do not add stored target-position or orientation fields: current calibrated geometry supplies those quantities. Retain the existing aligned-target descent rule and the earlier object-centered button and minimum-translation corrections."
}
